  <div class="section-wrapper" id="summary" >
    <div class="section-label"><h2>Summary</h2></div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <div class="cpu_description">With the <a href="/en/cpu-apple_m4">Apple M4</a>, Apple consolidates its lead in ARM-based desktop and mobile architecture. While the M3 made the jump to the 3 nm process (N3B), the M4 uses the refined second-generation 3 nm manufacturing (N3E). This not only allows higher clock speeds but above all a clearly improved energy efficiency under the same load.<br />
<br />
The M4 relies on a hybrid architecture with a total of 9 CPU cores, split into 3 performance cores and 6 efficiency cores. This variant is used, among others, in the iPad Pro with 256 or 512 GB of storage; we list the version with 4 performance cores separately as the <strong>Apple M4</strong> (10-CPU). The 3 "P-cores" clock at up to 4.46 GHz and the 6 "E-cores" at up to 2.85 GHz. The performance cores deliver strong single-core performance, while the efficiency cores are designed to use almost no energy for everyday system tasks.<br />
<br />
The excellent performance is confirmed by the Geekbench 6 results, in which the <strong>Apple M4</strong> achieves around 3,750 single-core points and about 13,300 points in the multi-core test. This puts the M4 ahead of many competing desktop CPUs, while it can still run in a fanless design.<br />
<br />
The graphics unit has 10 cores and continues features such as hardware-accelerated ray tracing and mesh shading. For gamers and 3D designers in particular, this means realistic lighting calculated in real time, something that was previously unthinkable on an SoC of this size.<br />
<br />
A key focus is the Neural Engine (NPU). With 16 cores it reaches a computing power of 38 TOPS (trillion operations per second). This makes the M4 well equipped for local AI workflows and Apple Intelligence.<br />
<br />
The <strong>Apple M4</strong> uses LPDDR5X-7500 memory, which results in a bandwidth of 120 GB/s. This is a critical factor for GPU performance and AI workloads, as both benefit greatly from fast data throughput.<ul><li><b>Manufactured using a 3nm process</b></li><li><b>Powerful integrated graphics</b> (Apple M4 (10 Core))</li><li><b>Strong benchmark results</b></li><li><b>High energy efficiency</b></li></ul> </div>
    </div>
    
  </div>
  



  <div class="section-wrapper" id="benchmarks">
    <div class="section-label" style="background-color: #189c37;"><h2>Benchmarks</h2></div>
    <div class="section-content">


        <div class="benchmark-overview">
            <div class="benchmark-overview-inner">
          
              <h3>Performance Overview<br>
                <span class="benchmark-overview-subtitle">Average performance across multiple benchmarks</span>
                <span class="info-icon" title="Cinebench 2024, Cinebench R23 & Geekbench 7">
                  <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                       xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true">
                    <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="#6B7280" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
                    <path d="M12 16v-4" stroke="#6B7280" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
                    <circle cx="12" cy="8" r="1" fill="#6B7280"/>
                  </svg>
                </span>
